  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the kitchen, Senya casually reveals that Lyova is a robot whose circuits jammed again. Then Barinov walks into the dining room to find Katya marrying Dmitry Nagiyev, making him realize he is trapped inside a dream.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Шеф, у нас Лёву заклинило.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, а что, Лёва робот?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Человек не смог бы работать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Скажи мне, а где Катя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "В зале, у неё свадьба.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какая ещё сегодня свадьба?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Папа, я очень люблю его.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Я всегда сильно любила его.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Витя, порадуйся за свою дочку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Значит, это всё совсем неправда!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Значит, это мне всё кажется.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "А ты ничего не помнишь?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Шеф, у нас Лёву заклинило.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, our Lyova jammed up.' Russian expresses 'having' with the preposition 'by' plus the genitive pronoun and 'jammed'. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, а что, Лёва робот?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, is Lyova a robot?' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'wait' and 'and' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Человек не смог бы работать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A human would not have been able to work.' In Russian, the idea of 'man' flows naturally into 'to work' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Скажи мне, а где Катя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Tell me, where is Katya?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'say' and finishing on 'Katya'. Senya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "В зале, у неё свадьба.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the hall, it is her wedding.' Senya anchors the line on 'hall', while 'wedding' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какая ещё сегодня свадьба?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What wedding today?' The grammar packs 'what a' and 'wedding' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Папа, я очень люблю его.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dad, I love him very much.' In Russian, the idea of 'dad' flows naturally into 'his' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Я всегда сильно любила его.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I always loved him deeply.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'his'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Витя, порадуйся за свою дочку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, be happy for your daughter.' The meaning hinges on 'to rejoice' working together with 'daughter' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Значит, это всё совсем неправда!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So all of this is completely untrue!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'so' before landing on 'untrue'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Значит, это мне всё кажется.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So I am just imagining all of this.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'so' and 'this' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "А ты ничего не помнишь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you not remember anything?' The grammar packs 'and' and 'remember' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
